{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rebound/positive slope (e.g., >~+0.4 €/t per day over 5 days), the model systematically under-forecasts levels from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors are modest in the first ~7–10 days, then become increasingly positive (true > forecast) through days ~15–30. Use a ramp correction (e.g., add ~0.5–1 €/t around day 10 rising to ~3–5 €/t by day 30 in strong uptrends).",
    "Lag in regime change: after a local bottom followed by a sharp reversal, forecasts behave like a lagged continuation of the prior weakness. Detect ‘bottom + breakout’ (price crosses above recent 10-day average after making a 10–20 day low) and accelerate the forecast path upward (steepen slope) rather than keeping it flat/mean-reverting.",
    "Mean-reversion too strong: the model pulls prices back toward the recent mean, but realized outcomes trend higher and stay elevated. Reduce mean-reversion strength when realized volatility is rising and the last 3–5 closes are above the 10-day mean.",
    "Late-horizon ceiling/flattening: forecasts tend to cap/flatten in the mid-60s while true outcomes reach upper-60s/near-70. If history shows a rebound into the mid-60s, allow higher terminal levels (raise the long-run anchor/terminal value).",
    "Spike-follow-through underpricing: after an upside impulse (several consecutive up days), the model expects partial giveback; realized prices show follow-through. Add a momentum carry component for days 5–20 after an upside impulse instead of fading it."
  ]
}
